{\*\generator Msftedit 5.41.21.2510;}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sl276\slmult1\lang11\f0\fs22 Clothes System / "Decorator" System for Planet Warfare (A game that PSRC was designed for)\line\line\b Summary:\b0\line\line This document talks about parameters for designing clothes, which ones to accept and which ones to never do under any circumstance. Also includes info on how are clothes structured regarding game engine performance and implementation ideas/instructions.\line\line\b Philosphy of cloth design:\b0\line\line In real life societies and cultures, some clothes/accessories people wear are due to indoctrination, such as, to buy someones love, attention and the benefits that brings.\line\line Its sadly a suicidal strategy, as being needy, which is what a person becomes when they use these strategies to "buy their way into heaven" or to "buy love", figuratively speaking obviously, only to end up having the very opposite effect by becoming unattractive at the very core. A dead shell that is also poisonous to others. Its a very serious issue.\line\line Whenever someone dresses up FOR someone instead of their own self:\line The dresser pays for it.\line The target pays for it.\line Everyone around pays for it.\line\line Its a lose-lose game. By "paying for it", I mean something bad will manifest.\line It may be direct damage (makeup is toxic, damages health. clothing is uncomfy, same thing.)\line It may be indirect damage (causes obsessions that lead to anorexia, depression or worse.)\line\line The damage can either occur to the wearer or the target its worn for to buy their love or it can occur by proxy to other people, which eventually will cascade down to someone either of the two original persons care for.\line\line Two question can reveal why "buying love" never works:\line 1. What do you want the person to do?\line 2. What do you want the person's reasons to be when they do it?\line\line With question 1, it seems like it could work, but question 2 reveals that it never does.\line\line This whole thing is annoyingly subtle and nearly everything in the environment is trying to indoctrinate the person into believing that you need to buy love, as well as reinforcing it with phrases like "this is how it was, this is how it is, this is how it will always be", so its difficult to explain to anyone if they haven't observed this effect happen in a way that gives understanding that if it were to happen to them, which it could very well, it'd be really bad.\par
\par
Futhermore, there may be vested interests and pre-existing environments where some benefit from it at the expense of others that the users do not have a caring connection to. Meanwhile others have adapted to abusing others and greatly benefit from it, unable to see why they should stop, only to percieve pleas to change their behaviour as direct attacks against them, making them violent instead. Its really shitty.\line\line So.\line\line For this very reason, its is extremely important to terminate this trend at its source by replacing it with something better. Namely, creating a norm, an environment where clothes are meant to empower the person and please them and only them. Thats where real attractive beauty comes from anyway and it doesn't cost negative consequences to anyone involved.\line\par
What this means, is that the clothes are designed for personal functionality, comfort and pleasing aesthetics for the wearer, primarily and only. Never to buy affection. Never to compromise the wearers own physical needs, own mental needs, own health, own style, own comfort.\line\line\line\b What I should never do when designing new PSRC clothing pieces:\line\b0\line Anything that compromises comfort - NO\line Anything that compromises health - NO\line Anything that is meant to please others, but not the wearer - NO\line Anything that compromises the human needs of the wearer - NO\line\line This means things like high-heels, piercings, bras, corsets, club dresses, show-off hats, brand accessories, wigs, tattoos... and anything else that falls under the 4 NOs. Exceptions are if any of the mentioned are actually enjoyable to the wearer for the sake of it on their own terms, then ok. But more often than not, there usually is some form of indoctrination or ulterior motive that ultimately ends up the wearable article becoming a means to "buy your way into heaven". \par
\par
I am very much against the entire idea of "beauty requires sacrifice" ideology or reckless disregard for one's physical health. I'd rather have people attain beauty through methods that are healthy for themselves and people around them.\par
\par
In my experience, I've often noticed how the supposed "prettiness" or "coolness" of an outfit collapses rapidly when I learn of the negative traits that came as a cost of creating the outfit or from wearing it. These ugly truths ruin outfits to the point they become worthless, stupid and disgusting. Its a similar effect to discovering a beautiful apple only to find out its rotten on the inside.\par
\par
Let us all try to achieve beauty, coolness, prettiness and aesthetics in a way that is also healthy, sustainable and comfortable. Art can inspire real life, so I'd prefer to create clothing and accessory design for PSRC human characters that shows a better, healthy, positive world.\par
\par
In other words, make clothing for PSRC that considers what its like to create that garment ethically, sustainably, economically, creating things that are durable, stylish and useful. Think about how it feels to wear the article, how to put it on, what its like to have it worn if it existed in real life and if theres any part that would be awful about it that should be fixed with a better solution.\par
\par
Also considering what to do with an expired or broken garment is also important too, how should it be disposed off and how should its materials be reclaimed and reused? If billions of people use clothing that eventually degrades to the point where they need new clothes, there should be some thought put into how to prevent endlessly growing piles of useless trash that would pollute the land or be a nuisance in some other way. Its better to recycle it or repurpose it so that theres a closed cycle of how materials are used that will work out even in the span of thousands of years of human civilization.\line\line\line\b Humax x Clothes - Ingame Structure & Implementation:\b0\line\line Regular human characters are made from seperate segments (torso, head, upper/lower limbs, hands, feet) that are animated by a skeletal rig. Each body part has its own collision boundaries and character movement is a physics-force based ordeal. Such characters can also turn into physics ragdolls.\line\line Most normal clothes are created in segments for each body part they correspond to and are layered on top of them. The clothes, make up, mods, accessories and so on are all assembled one on top of the other in a layered fashion, and the engine flattens it all out into a composite part to make it lightweight performance wise and simplify the object implementation wise.\line\line\line\b Abstractive Chaos System:\b0\line\line Planet Warfare, a game I'm designing, is essentially a universe simulator, where everything is a giant seamless world with no transitions, loading screens or seperation of areas. I'm talking about play-space about the size of a galaxy, but is played at a the zoomed in local level of a 2D character platformer experience.\line\line Additionally, its very freeform and sandboxy, which in theory allows for rather performance intense situation to happen ingame, like thousands of objects colliding, planets exploding, solar systems being wiped out and other situation where you'd normally expect millions, billions and trillions calculations to take present to represent something like that.\par
\par
Problem is, computer capabilities are limited. I want the system to run at 60fps, preferably on an average PC, so the maximum amount of calculation per frame have a strict limit that the game's system must aim for.\line\line Thats where the Abstractive Chaos System comes in. Its a solution to this problem by causing certain elements of the game system to be abstracted, aka reduced in complexity, simplified to facilitate bottleneck situations as a regular thing. Basically:\line\line Lots of crazy shit happening = More chaos = More aggressive abstraction of game elements.\line\line As a quick example, large events such as planetary explosions prioritize the giant fireball from the exploding planet and shut down all physics and visual rendering for anything that is insignificant compared to it.\line\line Another one is lots of random objects that are colliding with one another in a giant mass = group them into a single blob object, so instead of calculating tiny collision physics for each tiny object, just call it a big mass of stuff that have XYZ objects listed inside it and apply a modifier that says they're colliding. When they seperate, the final result of the collision smashing is applied to the final product in a single simplified line.\line\line Now back to how this applies to clothing.\line\line For a human character, the basic character itself is already quite complex even naked. This complexity could be practically doubled by adding clothes.\line\line Just to break it down quickly:\line Naked human = Head 1x, Torso 1x, Leg 2x, Arm 2x, Foot 2x, Hand 2x.\par
\par
Thats 10 independent parts for each human. All those parts are attached and animated with a rig so its much more complex than a single sprite character like megaman or super mario would be. The human character is also a physics ragdoll too, so theres the performance cost of a physics engine aswell. Furthermore, theres also a shader to apply/change the skin color and skin overlay texture (like when being painted over, or burned, or covered in goo). Finally, theres also the control of saturation, contrast and brigthness of the sprites for ingame lighting needs, and also for distance and highlighting reasons as a character vanishes into the background the further they are (despite being a 2D platformer, the game uses the background as horizon that bends inwards towards the center).\line\line Lets consider a very clothed human: Hat, goggles, breathing mask, undershirt, armor vest, pants, kneepads, ammo belt, boots, underwear, socks, cape, hair, wrist PDA accessory and other decor accessories.\line\line All those worn pieces would be overlaid on the torso, head and limbs. Tight clothes, like shirts and pants would just be pasted on top and use the same physics as the limbs, but things like capes, scarfs and other loose clothes would require their own physics component to allow them to flap around.\line\line Techwise, the idea is to combine whatever clothes, accesories and decals are placed there, each on top the other, on top the torso/limb/head, flatten it them into a single image and use that to represent that particular body part permanently as a saved graphic that is henceforth used by the game until its edited again, starting from scratch using the naked base and baking a new sprite to replace the old one.\line\line Additionally, the 10 human body parts, now clothed, would be put into single atlas image to further simplify perfomance load to a single draw call for a human.\line\line For a few humans, say 10 at once on screen, far and close, all this is no problem. \par
\par
But what if there are thousands of lives on screen? What if I want to populate a whole city?\par
\par
\b This is where I need to figure out how is the chaos system supposed to simplify that. \par
- \b0 Reduced animations to nothing and flash freeze the current frame to represent a human that is too far or otherwise not relevant to the current moment of gameplay?\line - Reduce that image to a lower quality like a mipmap or an LOD model in commonly seen in 3D games?\line - Use temporary simplified substitutes sprites to represent humans in high chaos situations?\line - Are they symbolic sprites, generic template human sprites or mutated(shifted perspective) substitute sprites?\line\line\ul\b I only have explored these ideas but I haven't solved a comprehensive solution that I've settled on, since I have to consider a lot of other factors besides human characters. Planet Warfare is meant to be a game where you could have the equivalent experience of existing in a Gundam Universal Century style environments with the types of insane events that happen during it, meaning I need to support destructible terrain, megastructures, giant mobile bases and real-time physics enabled combat gameplay.\par
\par
My point with this document was to highlight some of design thinking that goes into PSRC as thats what this whole art system was intended for. Because its so organized, standardized and flexible, it allows the correct balance between complexity and simplicity when it comes to character customization that lets you go into fairly deep in details to craft the exact look you want, but not so convoluted that it becomes impossible to manage nor so restricted that it feels like there aren't any actual options but just lame templates.\ulnone\b0\par
